course: dictionary
lesson: 13765
title: Interrogative: the question words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An interrogative is used to ask a question. In grammar, an interrogative sentence is a question, and it ends with a question mark. «Where is the library?» is an interrogative sentence because it asks something.
explain: There are four sentence types: statement, question (interrogative), command and exclamation. «The sun is hot.» is a statement. «Is the sun hot?» is interrogative. «Close the window.» is a command. «What a hot day!» is an exclamation.
explain: Some question words are called interrogative pronouns: who, whom, whose, which and what. In «Whose bag is this?», the word whose asks who owns the bag. Question words like where, when, why and how are also interrogatives.
fact: Interrogative means used to ask a question.
fact: An interrogative sentence ends with a question mark (?).
fact: An interrogative is a question word, such as who, what, where, when, why, which or how.
fact: Interrogative pronouns are who, whom, whose, which and what.
fact: An interrogative look seems to ask a question without any words, as in «She gave him an interrogative look.»
exercise: Which sentence is interrogative? | *«Is the door closed?» | «Close the door.» | «The door is closed.» | «What a lovely door!»
exercise: Which word in «Whose coat is this?» is an interrogative pronoun? | this | is | *whose | coat
exercise: Mia says «Where is my pencil?» is a statement. What type of sentence is it? | *Interrogative (a question) | Statement | Exclamation | Command
test: Which word is an interrogative pronoun? | *whom | happy | quickly | under
test: Which sentence asks a question? | *«When does the bus leave?» | «I like pizza.» | «Wow, what a day!» | «Sit down now.»
exam: Which word in «Who is at the door?» is an interrogative pronoun? | at | door | *who | is
